* A DASD read -- the first test that does what DMKIOS actually does.
*
* Tests 5, 6 and 7 drove a 3215 console with ONE CCW.  That proved the
* channel-subsystem plumbing and almost nothing about channel programs.
* DMKIOS drives DASD, and a DASD read is a CHAIN: command chaining, a
* transfer-in-channel that loops, and a device returning status you must care
* about.  This is the shape of the thing CP's I/O supervisor exists to issue.
*
*     SEEK              07  CC    6 bytes BBCCHH
*     SEARCH ID EQUAL   31  CC    5 bytes CCHHR
*     TIC               08        back to the SEARCH
*     READ DATA         06  SLI  80 bytes
*
* THE SEARCH/TIC IDIOM IS WORTH UNDERSTANDING, NOT COPYING.  When the search
* matches, the device returns STATUS MODIFIER, which makes the channel SKIP
* the next CCW -- the TIC -- and fall through to the READ.  When it does not
* match, control reaches the TIC, which branches back to the search to try
* the next record on the track.  The loop is built out of a conditional skip,
* and the channel program is genuinely a program.  Tests 5-7 exercised none
* of command chaining, status modifier or TIC.
*
* IT NEEDS A SECOND DEVICE, SO IT NEEDS A SECOND CONFIG.  Copy herc.conf and
* append, keeping the console FIRST so it stays subchannel 0000:
*
*     0190   3350   scratch.cckd
*
* THE TARGET IS A SCRATCH VOLUME, DELIBERATELY.  A bare-metal test builds its
* own channel programs with no operating system to stop it addressing the
* wrong subchannel.  During development of test 5 a write command reached
* subchannel 0000 while a writable CE pack was attached, and only a command
* reject prevented damage.  So make your own volume and attach nothing else:
*
*     dasdinit -z scratch.cckd 3350 SCRTCH 10
*
* Ten cylinders, about 3 KB compressed, containing nothing but the standard
* labels.  NEVER point this at a CE pack.
*
* WHAT IT READS.  dasdinit writes a standard VOL1 label at cylinder 0, head
* 0, record 3 -- verified with cckddiag before a line of the test was
* written:
*
*     Track 0 COUNT CC=0 HH=0 R=3 KL=4 DL=80
*     R3 KEY  (4)  E5D6D3F1                       VOL1
*     R3 DATA (80) E5D6D3F1 E2C3D9E3 C3C84000...  VOL1SCRTCH
*
* So it asserts the data begins 'VOL1' AND that the serial is 'SCRTCH'.  The
* second matters: it proves the read reached THIS volume rather than matching
* four plausible bytes by luck.
*
* NEGATIVE CONTROLS -- BOTH RUN, AND THE SECOND TAUGHT SOMETHING
* --------------------------------------------------------------
*   r 21D6=0000000009   search for record 9, which does not exist
*                       -> 001106.  The device returns a unit check, so the
*                          search really is searching.
*   r 21D6=0000000001   search for record 1, which is IPL1 rather than VOL1
*                       -> 001109, the RESIDUAL COUNT, not the data check.
*
* The second was expected to fail on 'VOL1' and did not, for a good reason:
* record 1 is 24 bytes, so reading 80 transfers 24 and leaves a residual of
* 56.  SLI suppresses the incorrect-length INDICATION but not the count.  The
* residual assertion fired first and caught a real difference -- which is
* why it is in the test rather than being ceremony.
*
* HOW TO READ THE RESULT
*   00600B   pass.  Chain executed, CE|DE, zero residual, VOL1SCRTCH read.
*   001101   STSCH cc/=0 -- almost always the wrong subchannel number.  The
*            console must be listed FIRST in the config or the DASD is not
*            0001.  Check `devlist`.
*   001102   MSCH cc/=0.        001103  SSCH cc/=0.      001104  TSCH cc=3.
*   001105   poll exhausted -- a seek that never completed.
*   001106   device status not CE|DE.  Read IRB+8.  A unit check means the
*            chain reached the device and it objected; get the sense.
*   001107   subchannel status set.  Read IRB+9: channel program check
*            (X'20') usually means a bad CCW address or alignment.
*   001108   residual count high byte set -- a very short transfer.
*   001109   residual count low byte set -- see control 2 above.
*   001111   data is not 'VOL1'.  Dump READBUF at 0021E5.
*   001112   data is 'VOL1' but the serial is not SCRTCH -- wrong volume.
*   000DED   program check -- read the old PSW at X'150'.
*
* KEY ADDRESSES
*   chain   0021B0   SEEK, SEARCH, TIC, READ -- contiguous, doubleword aligned
*   SRCHID  0021D6   CCHHR, 5 bytes -- poke this for the controls above
*   READBUF 0021E5   80 bytes
*   IRB     00216C   +8 devstat  +9 substat  +10 residual count
*   SSIDW   002110   00010001 -- subchannel 0001
*
* Built by build/dasd1.py.
*
r 0=0008000000002000
